.nh
.TH "PVEFORGE" "1" "Sep 2026" "pveforge" "pveforge manual"

.SH NAME
pveforge-doctor - Report which privileges `vm destroy` needs and which the token holds


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBpveforge doctor <target-id> [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Report, for `vm destroy`, each privilege the token needs and whether it holds it.
It changes nothing: the token over REST, GET requests only, no SSH, no write to
the roster.

.PP
Each row is held, missing, unknown or not checked. The verdict is what the
verb's own read answers, not the permission tree: Sys.Audit on the node is
GET /nodes/<node>/status (200 held, 403 missing, anything else unknown), and
Sys.Audit on / is the cluster member list the retry reads. The permission tree
is printed beside it as a hint only.

.PP
The privileges on a VM (VM.PowerMgmt, VM.Allocate) are checked only when you
name one with --vmid; without it those rows are "not checked".

.PP
Exit status: 0 only when every row is held. 4 when any row is missing, unknown
or not checked: nothing was dispatched, so pass --vmid, or grant what is
missing (a missing Sys.Audit row prints `pveforge acl enable-vm-destroy <target-id>`).
1 is doctor's own failure (roster, passphrase, usage).


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for doctor

.PP
\fB--lock-wait\fP=0s
	how long to wait for another pveforge process's lock on this object before giving up (0 = the 15m0s default; at most 1h0m0s). The default exceeds the 10m0s ceiling on waiting for a PVE task because a legitimate lock holder may be in such a wait the whole time

.PP
\fB-o\fP, \fB--output\fP="kv"
	output format: "kv" or "json"

.PP
\fB--roster\fP=""
	path to the roster file (overrides PVEFORGE_ROSTER and the default ./pveforge.toml)

.PP
\fB--vmid\fP=""
	also check the privileges on this VM (a canonical decimal vmid)


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBpveforge(1)\fP
